In the United States, the onset of adolescent sexuality is often indicated by the first instance of consensual sexual intercourse. This event may occur much earlier than the biological changes associated with puberty, and it may vary significantly among individuals and cultures. For example, some research has shown that onset of coitus occurs earlier for adolescents in lower socioeconomic status (SES) groups as compared to those in higher SES groups, possibly due to greater exposure to early marriage norms and economic pressures. Other sociocultural factors, such as geographic location and ethnic background, have also been shown to influence the timing of the onset of sexual activity.
